### What is the Repository pattern?

The Repository pattern follows the Dependency Inversion Principle: high-level modules (service) depend on abstractions (repository interface), not low-level modules (JPA implementation). The service never imports JPA, EntityManager, or SQL.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

// Repository interface: defines WHAT, not HOW
public interface TradeRepository {
    List<Trade> findSettledByAccountId(String accountId);
    Optional<Trade> findById(String tradeId);
    Trade save(Trade trade);
    void deleteById(String tradeId);
}
// Service depends on THIS interface.
// Does not know about JPA, SQL, or MongoDB.
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.repository.TradeRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class SettlementService {

    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;  // interface, not implementation!

    public SettlementService(TradeRepository tradeRepo) {
        this.tradeRepo = tradeRepo;
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public BigDecimal calculateSettledTotal(String accountId) {
        return tradeRepo.findSettledByAccountId(accountId).stream()
            .map(Trade::getAmount)
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
    }
}
```

### How does Spring Data implement the Repository pattern?

With Spring Data, you define an interface and Spring generates the implementation class at runtime. Method names are parsed into JPQL queries automatically.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import java.util.List;

// Spring Data auto-generates the implementation!
public interface JpaTradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {
    List<Trade> findByAccountIdAndStatus(String accountId, String status);
    // Spring parses the method name and generates:
    // SELECT t FROM Trade t WHERE t.accountId = ?1 AND t.status = ?2
    // No SQL written by the developer!
}
```

### How is Repository different from DAO?

Both abstract data access. DAO is more data-oriented and traditionally exposes CRUD at a lower level. Repository is DDD-oriented (Domain-Driven Design) – it acts as a collection of domain objects.

| Aspect | Repository | DAO |
|--------|-----------|-----|
| Origin | Domain-Driven Design | Core J2EE Patterns |
| Abstraction | Collection of domain objects | CRUD on tables |
| Implementation | Spring auto-generates | Developer writes manually |
| SQL visibility | Hidden (derived queries) | Often visible (JDBC, SQL) |
| Technology | Spring Data (JPA, Mongo, Redis) | Typically JDBC or JPA |

```java
package com.bank.dao;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;
import java.util.List;

// DAO: lower-level, SQL visible
@Repository
public class TradeDao {

    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;

    public TradeDao(JdbcTemplate jdbc) {
        this.jdbc = jdbc;
    }

    public List<Trade> findSettled(String accountId) {
        return jdbc.query(
            "SELECT * FROM trades WHERE account_id = ? AND status = 'SETTLED'",
            new TradeRowMapper(), accountId);
        // SQL is visible. DAO knows about JDBC.
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import java.util.List;

// Repository: higher-level, no SQL
public interface TradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {
    List<Trade> findByAccountIdAndStatus(String accountId, String status);
    // No SQL! Spring generates the query from the method name.
}
```

### Why keep queries out of controllers and services?

Controllers handle HTTP concerns (request parsing, response formatting). Services handle business logic (calculations, rules). Repositories handle data access (queries, persistence). Mixing these violates single responsibility.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.service.TradeService;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.util.List;

@RestController
public class TradeController {

    private final TradeService tradeService;

    public TradeController(TradeService tradeService) {
        this.tradeService = tradeService;
    }

    // WRONG: query in controller
    // @GetMapping("/trades")
    // public List<Trade> getTrades(@RequestParam String accountId,
    //                              EntityManager em) {
    //     return em.createQuery(
    //         "SELECT t FROM Trade t WHERE t.accountId = :id", Trade.class)
    //         .setParameter("id", accountId).getResultList();
    //     // Controller knows about EntityManager + JPQL. Tight coupling!
    // }

    // CORRECT: query in repository, logic in service
    @GetMapping("/trades")
    public List<Trade> getTrades(@RequestParam String accountId) {
        return tradeService.getTradesForAccount(accountId);
        // Controller knows NOTHING about persistence.
    }
}
```

### How does the Repository pattern help with testing?

With a repository interface, service unit tests mock the repository. No database needed. Tests run in milliseconds.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.repository.TradeRepository;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;
import org.mockito.InjectMocks;
import org.mockito.Mock;
import org.mockito.junit.jupiter.MockitoExtension;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.when;

@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class SettlementServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private TradeRepository tradeRepo;

    @InjectMocks
    private SettlementService service;

    @Test
    void shouldCalculateSettledTotal() {
        Trade t1 = new Trade("TRD-001", "RELIANCE", new BigDecimal("100000"));
        Trade t2 = new Trade("TRD-002", "HDFC", new BigDecimal("200000"));

        when(tradeRepo.findSettledByAccountId("SAV2001"))
            .thenReturn(List.of(t1, t2));

        BigDecimal total = service.calculateSettledTotal("SAV2001");

        assertThat(total).isEqualByComparingTo("300000");
        // NO database! Mock returns test data.
        // Test runs in 10ms.
    }
}
```

